;; re-fetched origin/main registry.edn (via git-trees/blobs API)
;; immediately before this test-file edit, not assumed:
;; {:total 649 :spec 194 :blueprint 0 :implemented 455} PLUS this
;; promotion's own cloud-itonami-isic-0128 (Growing of spices,
;; aromatic, drug and pharmaceutical crops -- Wave 3's last genuine
;; implementation gap, ADR-2780000128) landed just before this
;; test-file edit: live-recomputed via
;; `(kotoba.industry/maturity-summary)` against a freshly
;; re-fetched origin/main registry.edn (via git-trees/blobs API)
;; immediately before this test-file edit, not assumed:
;; {:total 649 :spec 193 :blueprint 0 :implemented 456}.
;; 456 -> 458: re-bumped to the current registry (total 650 / implemented 458,
;; after the ISIC 649 eKYC promotion and siblings) to unblock CI on
;; gen-actor-business-model -- pre-existing drift, this count routinely
;; moves under concurrent fleet promotions (see the history above).
;; A FLOOR, not an equality. This was an exact count and it could not
      ;; stay green: a live fleet promotes :spec -> :implemented continuously,
      ;; so the number moved under the test and every agent that promoted an
      ;; industry had to stop and re-bump it. The history above records at
      ;; least five such bumps (448 -> 451 -> 453 -> 455 -> 456 -> 458), and
      ;; one of the comments says it plainly: this aggregate "legitimately
      ;; drifts multiple times within minutes under concurrent fleet load".
      ;;
      ;; CI was red on main from that drift alone. A floor keeps the assertion
      ;; that actually matters -- industries must not silently LOSE
      ;; :implemented -- while tolerating the fleet moving forward, which is
      ;; the fleet working. The partition invariant above is the real
      ;; structural check and is unaffected either way.
      (is (<= 458 (:implemented m))
          "implemented count must never regress below the recorded floor"))))

