re any proposal for it may commit or escalate, re-derived from the store every time, never from proposal self-report), contract-unverified (the client packaging-service contract the proposal is working under must ALSO exist AND be independently :registered?/:verified?, checked unconditionally on every op -- contract packaging is always performed under a specific client engagement, never as a standalone facility action), supplier-unverified (for :coordinate-supply-order only, the named packaging-material supplier must independently resolve to a :registered?/:verified? supplier record, the same ground-truth discipline reapplied to the supply-chain counterparty), effect-not-propose, and scope-exclusion (folds in op-not-allowed) that permanently blocks any proposal touching directly finalizing a package-integrity-safety clearance (certifying a package/batch as tamper-evident, clearing a batch for release, issuing a package-integrity safety clearance) OR a food/pharma-labeling-compliance sign-off (approving/signing off on food/pharma label accuracy, declaring a label regulatory-compliant) -- scope-excluded terms are deliberately phrased as the finalization/execution ACTION (\"certified the batch as tamper-evident\", \"signed off on the food safety labeling compliance\"), never a bare noun (\"tamper\"/\"seal\"/\"label\"/\"contamination\"/\"integrity\") -- this namespace's own :flag-quality-concern default proposal legitimately discusses seal/tamper-evidence/label/contamination concerns as observations, and its own printed :op keyword literally contains the substring \"quality\", so a bare-noun term list would have self-tripped this actor's own core happy path on every single run -- avoided from the start, verified by a dedicated regression test (default-mock-advisor-proposals-never-self-trip-scope-exclusion in governor_test.clj) asserting all four default proposal generators never trip :scope-excluded or :op-not-allowed; :flag-quality-concern ALWAYS escalates to human sig